{
  "dom_id": "step-q313453",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-16x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}$ on the curve at the point $(1,4),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-16x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4},$ $x=1,$ and $y=4,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
